  "surprise": "P50-family DeepSeek-V3 MTP weight schedule (lambda 0.3 until frac 10/14.8 = 0.676 of the 300 s pretraining, then 0.1; handoff model identical to 136's 4,800,000 params) scored 656/654/656 on seeds 0/1/2 vs 136-fable's 655/673/646: exact means 655.33 vs 658.00, -2.67, paired +1/-19/+10, inside the registered -35..+30 range and just below its central +3. Discard (not strictly above). Surprise: the registered mechanism check came out as predicted and the metric still did not move. The RL start was clearly stronger: round-1 targets solved 678/622/709 (136: 577/529/662), round-1 sample acc 0.256/0.181/0.253 (136: 0.137/0.135/0.210), and round-4 targets 1153/1134/1147 (136: 1102/1114/1124, mean +31). Transfer from those RL solves to dev >=7 got worse: eval/round-4 ratio 0.569/0.577/0.572 vs 136's 0.594/0.604/0.575. This mirrors 136's own surprise, a weaker start with better transfer, so across 129 -> 136 -> 143 a later-stage MTP signal trades round-1 harvest for dev transfer. Round-1 and round-4 target counts are not proxies for the metric. The seed spread was unusually tight (654-656). Pretraining matched prediction except for one fast pod: 3677/3405/3403 steps (136: 3370/3351/3367), final main val loss 0.0894/0.0842/0.0889 (minima 0.0773/0.0780/0.0755; 136: 0.086/0.083/0.079), held-out greedy 0.970/0.9735/0.9645, totals 1482/1560/1617 s. The unweighted mtp_loss did not rise after the switch as predicted (~0.056-0.057 at 210-240 s, 0.054-0.056 at 300 s, same as 136's 0.0559 at 240 s and 0.0553 at 300 s): at this point the t+2 target is learned and the weight mainly changes how hard the trunk is pulled toward it. Not tested: whether a HIGHER late weight improves transfer further at the cost of the start.",
